      ],
      "text": "Dose (nonvalvular AF):"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " apixaban 5 mg PO twice daily, or 2.5 mg twice daily with at least 2 of age ≥ 80, weight ≤ 60 kg or serum creatinine ≥ 1.5 mg/dL; rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily with the evening meal (CrCl ≤ 50 mL/min: 15 mg once daily)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Azole antifungals and protease inhibitors raise oral Xa inhibitor levels; certain antiseizure drugs and rifampin lower them."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Reversal:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " andexanet alfa (Andexxa) was indicated for life-threatening or uncontrolled bleeding on rivaroxaban or apixaban; US commercial sales ended December 22, 2025. If andexanet alfa is unavailable, 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate may be considered."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
